<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=general-anaesthesia">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Medicine and health</p><h1 class="enc-title">General anaesthesia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1804">1804</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as general anesthesia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">General anaesthesia is a drug-induced loss of consciousness from which a patient cannot be roused even by pain; the first successful surgery on record under it was performed by the Japanese surgeon Hanaoka Seishū on 14 November 1804. Attempts to produce it appear in the writings of ancient peoples from the Sumerians to the Chinese. Together with antiseptic technique, its development in the 19th century allowed the transition to modern surgery, which pain had made a last resort, and in the 20th century tracheal intubation, monitoring and new agents made it safer.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/general-anaesthesia.jpg" alt="General anaesthesia" width="320" height="461" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hanaoka Seishu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Image_from_%22Surgical_Casebook%22_by_Hanaoka_Seishu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1804</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Japan</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Hanaoka Seishū · <a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Who deserves the most credit for general anaesthesia is debated.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=general-anaesthesia">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
